quest long_scroll_test

title "Long Scroll Test"

node start
You push open a heavy iron door and step into a corridor that seems longer than the building itself. The ceiling hangs low enough that every breath sounds close, warm, and immediate, while the lamps along the walls burn with a soft yellow light that refuses to reach the corners. Dust drifts slowly in front of your eyes like tiny sparks in still air, and every footstep sends a thin echo down the stone, forward and backward at the same time. A row of old wooden lockers runs along the left side, each scratched with initials, arrows, dates, warnings, and quiet jokes that no one else was meant to read. On the right side, long windows are sealed from the inside with dark paint, but cracks in the coating let in narrow lines of daylight that divide the floor into bright and shadowed strips. A faded emergency map hangs crooked near your shoulder, and somebody has drawn a new route on top of it with a red marker: turn, turn, turn, then a circle around a room with no label. You pause, listening, and realize there is a second rhythm under your own movement, as if somewhere far ahead another person is matching your pace, stopping when you stop, continuing when you continue.
The corridor bends gently and reveals an old notice board covered with layered paper: schedules, lists, handwritten reminders, pieces torn away, and a final page pinned in all four corners that simply says, in careful block letters, "If you came this far, do not hurry." The sentence should feel comforting, but the longer you read it, the more it sounds like an instruction from someone who already knows your next choice. Beneath the board lies a metal box with its lid half-open. Inside you find a key wrapped in cloth, a small flashlight with weak batteries, and a card with three words stamped in blue ink: LOOK BACK SLOWLY. You do not follow the card immediately. Instead you stand still and count to five, then ten, then fifteen. The air carries a smell of machine oil, rain-soaked concrete, and a trace of smoke that might be old or new. Somewhere above, unseen pipes click and settle. Somewhere behind, a door closes with perfect calm. Nothing rushes, yet everything insists that this exact moment is important, and that what you choose next will decide whether the corridor is just a corridor or the beginning of something you will remember for years.
> Walk to the archive door
-> archive
> Go down to the maintenance tunnel
-> tunnel

node archive
The archive room opens with a dry mechanical snap, and immediately the sound around you changes. Outside the corridor your steps echoed; inside the archive, every movement is absorbed by paper, cloth bindings, and rows of shelves packed so tightly that they form narrow canyons. The room is much larger than expected, stretching deeper than your flashlight can cleanly reveal. Label plates mark sections by year, then by district, then by street, then by building code, then by renovation phase, as if someone believed all uncertainty could be conquered by enough categories. You trace your fingers across boxes that carry brittle stickers, cracked tape, and signatures of clerks long retired. There are maps folded to impossible precision, maintenance logs filled in with tidy blue handwriting, and incident reports with lines blacked out so heavily that the page texture changed under the marker. On one table sits a recorder with a blinking red light. It should not have power, yet it does. When you press play, a voice speaks your full name, then reads your arrival time, then says: "If you are hearing this, the old route still works. Do not trust the shortest path."
You place the recorder down and notice that the table surface is covered with tiny compass roses etched into the wood, each pointing a slightly different direction. In the far corner, a clock without numbers ticks once every few seconds, never the same interval twice. A bundle of keys hangs near the clock, and each key has a tag with a short phrase: "Roof hatch," "Pump room," "West gate," "Return route." You test the archive window and find it sealed shut by age and paint, but cool air leaks through the frame, carrying distant rain and the low hum of city traffic that sounds impossibly far away. On a shelf at eye level, between two binders, someone hid a narrow notebook. The first page contains a simple line: "Write only what you can verify." The next pages are full of short entries in different handwriting styles, all describing the same hallway from different decades, and all ending with the same final sentence: "I left by the long way, and that is why I came back."
> Return to the corridor
-> end
> Take the return route key and leave
-> end

node tunnel
The maintenance tunnel begins with concrete stairs and a handrail polished smooth by countless hands. The air turns colder with each step, and the smell changes from dust and paper to wet metal, old paint, and mineral water. At the bottom, a long passage runs beneath the building, lined with cables wrapped in cloth sleeves and pipes marked with fading color bands. Condensation gathers on the ceiling and drops at irregular intervals, each drop striking the floor with a sound that is sharp, then immediately swallowed by the tunnel's depth. Your flashlight beam catches warning signs in three languages, half of them peeled away, all of them urgent. You pass a junction box that hums softly and a service door chained from the outside, then another service door chained from the inside. Far ahead, a green emergency light blinks with stubborn regularity, making shadows pulse across the walls in a slow rhythm. Someone painted arrows near the floor, but many arrows point in opposite directions, and some loop back toward where you started.
You stop near a maintenance cart loaded with coiled rope, cracked gloves, and a thermos that is still warm. A map is clipped to the side of the cart, drawn by hand over an official blueprint. The hand-drawn layer is practical and blunt: "quiet floor here," "watch water level," "no signal," "do not run." As you follow the map, the tunnel narrows and then widens into a circular chamber with a grated platform in the center. Through the grate you can see water moving below, black and smooth, reflecting the emergency light in trembling green lines. Along the wall are three levers, all labeled incorrectly by old repairs; someone scratched better labels beneath them: "drain," "bypass," "open north." You hear a train overhead that never appears on city schedules, then the sound fades and leaves a ringing silence. In that silence you understand that the tunnel is less a hidden shortcut and more a test of whether you can move carefully when every sign tries to push you into haste.
> Climb back upstairs
-> end
> Follow the green light to the exit hatch
-> end

node end
You leave the area with more questions than answers, but the path behind you is now clear enough to retrace.
